@* The thin proportion bar the app reports a share with: a row's win rate, a catalogue's
   coverage, the games that ended at one terror band, how much of the community's play a
   difficulty band accounts for.

   .meter / .meter-fill in site.css name the track and the fill; this names the *pair*, which
   is the part that was actually being repeated — six call sites each wrote the same two nested
   divs and each computed its own width string. They had already come apart on the one thing a
   proportion bar cannot afford: three clamped their percentage and three did not, so a share
   that arithmetic could push past a hundred (a rounding artefact, a denominator narrowed by a
   filter) would have run the fill straight out of its track. The clamp lives here now, once,
   and no caller has to remember it.

   The fill's colour stays with the caller, because the colour is what the bar is saying —
   jade for coverage, ember for defeats, WinTone's ramp for a rate, the band's own colour for a
   terror level. It arrives either as a utility (FillClass) or as a value (FillColor), since
   half of these are painted from the palette and half from a colour the data carries. *@

<div class="meter @Class">
    <div class="meter-fill @FillClass" style="width:@(Percent)%;@(FillColor is null ? null : $" background-color: {FillColor};")"></div>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The share the fill represents, 0–100. Clamped, so a caller's arithmetic cannot
    /// push the fill out of its track.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public double Value { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The fill's colour as a utility — <c>bg-accent-500</c> for coverage,
    /// <c>bg-red-400</c> for a defeat count. Use <see cref="FillColor"/> instead where the
    /// colour is a value the data carries.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? FillClass { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The fill's colour as a value, for the bars painted from something other than the
    /// palette — <see cref="Games.WinTone"/>'s ramp, a terror band's own colour.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? FillColor { get; set; }

    /// <summary>How the host lays the track out: the taller <c>h-2</c> the panel-scale meters
    /// take, the <c>flex-1</c> that lets one share a row with the figure beside it, the space
    /// above it.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Whole percent — the fill is a bar, not a measurement, and a fractional width only ever
    /// renders as a longer style attribute. Invariant because it is going into CSS; see
    /// <see cref="BrowserNumber"/> for what that costs when it is forgotten.
    /// </summary>
    private string Percent => Math.Clamp(Value, 0, 100).ToInvariant("F0");
}
